Z||000020||Cliff Pennock||09-05-2001||01:16 PM||||You can buy the "quarter trick" at any magician shop for a few bucks. The levitation trick is very simple also. What you see Blaine do on tv, is <B>not</B> the trick itself. In the show, it's a clever piece of editing. He does the "normal" levitation trick, then shows and tells the same people how it is done on stage (he puts on a harness, and he is being lifted by a crane). So by some clever editing, you'll see Blaine levitate (at that point he has the harness on and the audience in reality isn't impressed), then they show you the audience's reaction to his <B>original</B> trick. Like I said, it's presented as if it is a documentary of a street artist, while in reality, he has all the help he wants (not a lot of people carry harnesses around and have a crane at their disposal).||||reg||1
Z||000021||Urchin||09-05-2001||01:30 PM||||That's kind of disappointing to think about it as simply a lot of editing.  Cheap really. There were a few of the tricks that I had a pretty good idea how he did it.  Mainly the card tricks, and a couple of the coin ones.  One of the others that just came to mind was the "pick a number/card (in your head), and I'll tell you what you chose"  That's just plain cheesy if that was completely staged <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.   ||||reg||1
Z||000022||Cliff Pennock||09-05-2001||01:57 PM||||The "pick a number/card" stuff is real easy to do. There are many variations and they all use some simple tricks. The pick a number he did was one of the most simpler variations. This is how it goes:<P><B>Pick an even number between 0-100.</B><BR>The person thinks of a number, for instance 24<BR><B>It must be higher than 50 and both digits must be even.</B><BR>This is where it all of a sudden gets a lot more difficult for the person. In their head<BR>they'll start counting in steps of ten till the first even number above 50 - "10, 20, 30, 40, 50, <B>60</B>". Your brain will continue this trend to find the next even number which is 8. So the number 4 out of 5 people will give is <B>68</B>.<P>Like I said, there are lots and lots of variations to this, and you'll be able to make a correct prediction about 80% of the time. The people they showed on tv were really people off the street. But ofcourse they only showed those people of which he "predicted" the number/card correctly.<BR>||||reg||1
